misc: hy310-board-mgr: tachometer via GPIO IRQ, no surprise poweroff

Three changes that have to land before this driver can be enabled on the
HY310.

1. The tachometer stops bypassing gpiolib and pinctrl. The driver ioremap'd
   0x02000000 itself, wrote the PIO CFG register behind pinctrl's back and
   polled the DAT register from a 100 us hrtimer -- 10000 wakeups per second,
   forever, which keeps the CPU out of any deep idle state. Its stated reason
   was "H713 EINT does not work for extended PH pins (PH11-PH19); function 6
   disconnects the input path". That was never a property of the SoC: the pin
   tables carried the H616 EINT mux value 0x6, which is not the EINT function
   here. Patch 0140 set the correct value 0xe and restored the mux switch on
   IRQ request (measured on PL4: mux 0 never raised EINT, mux 0xe raised it on
   every edge). PH17 carries SUNXI_FUNCTION_IRQ_BANK(0xe, 6, 17) and PH is an
   IRQ bank, so a plain GPIO interrupt works. Use devm_fwnode_gpiod_get() +
   gpiod_to_irq() + devm_request_irq() on the rising edge and count there.
   If the pin has no interrupt, say so and leave fan1_input unavailable
   rather than falling back to a poll.

   Pulses per revolution becomes the common "pulses-per-revolution" property
   (the name pwm-fan uses), defaulting to 2 -- rising edges only on a standard
   3-wire fan. The old hard-coded 4 contradicted its own comment ("both edges")
   while the code counted rising edges only. The absolute RPM is still
   unverified against a reference tachometer.

2. The thermal emergency poweroff is disarmed by default. hy310_ntc_work_fn()
   called kernel_power_off() after 50 cycles above temp_poweroff, and the DTS
   sets that to 60 C. The lradc-table behind the conversion comes from the
   vendor DTS of a board whose thermal design we have not verified; a
   mis-calibrated curve powers the device off at random. It is now behind the
   thermal_shutdown module parameter, default off: the driver reports and
   warns, and arming it is a deliberate act once temp1_input is known to be
   truthful. This is not a regression -- today the driver is not built at all,
   so the device runs with no thermal reporting whatsoever.

   The dead, fully commented-out fan-stall shutdown and its no_rpm_shutdown
   parameter go away with it. A stall is now reported once per crossing and
   never acted on: this board's fan is an unregulated on/off load on the
   shared PB5 rail (see patch 0030), so the driver cannot restart it, and
   powering the device off would trade a warning for an outage.

3. A fan without a PWM is a supported configuration. duty-array-policy1
   becomes optional, hwmon stops exposing pwmN when there is no PWM behind it,
   and the thermal cooling device is only registered when a fan can actually
   be throttled -- an unregulated fan's set_cur_state does nothing, and a
   cooling device that does nothing lies to the thermal core. The HY310's fan
   is exactly that case.

Not hardware-tested yet: the tachometer IRQ on the PH bank. Only PL4 (bank L)
has ever raised a GPIO interrupt on this board. If PH turns out not to work,
the driver degrades to temperature-only and says so in the log.

